# Local adjustments to the stack. Compose picks up a docker-compose.override.yml
#   automatically next to docker-compose.yml, and git ignores it - so your changes
#   survive every `git pull` without conflicts. Copy this example to get started:
#
#     cp docker-compose.override.yml.dist docker-compose.override.yml
#
# This file is for changing settings of the services that docker-compose.yml already
#   defines. Optional scenarios are not loaded here - set COMPOSE_FILE in .env for
#   that, see .env.dist. If you do, append this file to that list as well: setting
#   COMPOSE_FILE turns off the automatic pickup of docker-compose.override.yml, so
#   your changes here would otherwise go unused without any warning.
#
# Files that this override points at - certificates, config snippets, scripts - can live
#   in local/, which git ignores as well (see local/README.md). Refer to them as
#   ./local/...; relative paths resolve against this directory.
#
# Compose's `include:` looks like it would do the same job as COMPOSE_FILE, but it is
#   not an override mechanism: it copies a scenario's resources into the stack instead
#   of layering them on top, so a scenario cannot undo anything docker-compose.yml
#   sets. The reverse proxy scenarios would silently keep nginx published on the host,
#   and relative paths inside a scenario would resolve against scenarios/ rather than
#   against this directory.
#
# Everything below is an inactive example - uncomment what you need. The x- key is only
#   there to keep this file valid while nothing else is uncommented, Compose ignores
#   it. Do not drop it: a file with nothing but comments is accepted by current Compose
#   versions, but fails on the 2.23.1 this stack supports.

x-local-overrides: Placeholder, so that this file stays valid while everything below is commented out.

